1. Java Program                                  (10 Marks)
import java.sql.*;
public class PreparedStmtDemo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
try {
Class.forName("com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver");
Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(
"jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/studentdb",
"root", "password");
PreparedStatement ps =
con.prepareStatement("INSERT INTO Student VALUES(?, ?)");
ps.setInt(1, 101);
ps.setString(2, "John");
ps.executeUpdate();
PreparedStatement ps1 =
con.prepareStatement("SELECT * FROM Student");
ResultSet rs = ps1.executeQuery();
while (rs.next()) {
System.out.println(rs.getInt(1) + " " +
rs.getString(2));
}
con.close();
} catch (Exception e) {
System.out.println(e);
}     }  }
2. Step-by-Step Explanation               (3 Marks)
Load Driver – Loads the MySQL JDBC driver.
Create Connection – Connects the Java application to the database.
Create PreparedStatement – Uses SQL with ? placeholders.
Set Values – Assigns values using setInt() and setString().
Execute Update – Inserts data using executeUpdate().
Execute Query – Retrieves records using executeQuery().
Process ResultSet – Reads and displays the retrieved data.
Close Connection – Releases database resources.
Why PreparedStatement is Preferred
Security: Prevents SQL Injection attacks.
Performance: SQL statement is precompiled, improving execution speed.
Maintainability: Easier to read, reuse, and modify than Statement.

1. Problem – Race Condition                    (3 Marks)
Multiple threads access the same BankAccount object simultaneously.
Without synchronization, the read–modify–write operation on balance may overlap.
This causes race conditions, lost updates, and an incorrect final balance.
2. Unsynchronized Program                     (3 Marks)
class UnsafeAccount {
int balance = 1000;
void withdraw(int amt) {
if (balance >= amt) {
try { Thread.sleep(5); }
catch (InterruptedException e) { }
balance -= amt;
}      }  }
Key Point:
Both threads may check balance >= amt before either updates the balance.
This can lead to overdrawing and an incorrect balance because the critical section is not synchronized.
